"https://mobile.twitter.com" rel="nofollow">Twitter Web App</a>', 'note_tweet': {'is_expandable': True, 'note_tweet_results': {'result': {'id': 'Tm90ZVR3ZWV0OjE4Nzc3Nzk2MDA3Njk2NzkzNjA=', 'text': 'From first principles, fenbendazole and ivermectin could potentially affect cancer through several key mechanisms:\nMicrotubule Destabilization\nBoth drugs, particularly fenbendazole, act as microtubule destabilizing agents. This mechanism:\n•Disrupts mitotic spindle formation, preventing cancer cell division\n•Induces cell cycle arrest, particularly in the G2/M phase\n•May trigger apoptosis in cancer cells unable to complete mitosis\nMetabolic Disruption\nFenbendazole has been shown to interfere with cancer cell metabolism:\n•Inhibits glucose uptake by downregulating GLUT transporters\n•Impairs hexokinase activity, disrupting glycolysis\n•May lead to energetic stress in cancer cells reliant on aerobic glycolysis\nApoptosis Induction\nBoth drugs can promote programmed cell death in cancer cells:\n•Activate intrinsic apoptosis pathways via mitochondrial damage\n•Upregulate pro-apoptotic proteins like Bax and downregulate anti-apoptotic proteins like Bcl-2\n•Increase caspase activity, particularly caspase-3 and caspase-7\nCell Signaling Modulation\nIvermectin in particular has been shown to interfere with multiple signaling pathways crucial for cancer progression:\n•Inhibits the Wnt/β-catenin pathway\n•Suppresses the PI3K/Akt/mTOR pathway\n•Modulates the STAT3 pathway\nOxidative Stress Induction\nBoth drugs may increase reactive oxygen species (ROS) in cancer cells:\n•Elevated ROS can damage cellular components and trigger apoptosis\n•Cancer cells may be more vulnerable to ROS-induced damage due to their altered redox state\nImmunomodulation\nIvermectin has demonstrated immunomodulatory effects that could enhance anti-tumor immunity:\n•Promotes immunogenic cell death, releasing tumor antigens\n•Enhances T cell infiltration into tumors\n•Modulates the tumor microenvironment to favor anti-tumor immune responses\nMultidrug Resistance Reversal\nIvermectin may help overcome multidrug resistance in cancer cells:\n•Inhibits P-glycoprotein, a key mediator of drug efflux\n•Could potentially enhance the efficacy of other chemotherapeutic agents\nThese mechanisms, acting in concert, could theoretically impair cancer cell survival, proliferation, and metastasis while enhancing the body’s natural anti-tumor defenses. However, it’s crucial to note that most of these effects have been observed in preclinical studies, and their clinical relevance in human cancer treatment remains to be established through rigorous clinical trials.', 'entity_set': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': []}, 'richtext': {'richtext_tags': []}, 'media': {'inline_media': []}}}}, 'legacy': {'bookmark_count': 0, 'bookmarked': False, 'created_at': 'Fri Jan 10 18:08:38 +0000 2025', 'conversation_id_str': '1877525664040988721', 'display_text_range': [14, 289], 'entities': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'timestamps': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': [{'id_str': '69156861', 'name': 'Chief Nerd', 'screen_name': 'TheChiefNerd', 'indices': [0, 13]}]}, 'favorite_count': 0, 'favorited': False, 'full_text': '@TheChiefNerd From first principles, fenbendazole and ivermectin could potentially affect cancer through several key mechanisms:\nMicrotubule Destabilization\nBoth drugs, particularly fenbendazole, act as microtubule destabilizing agents. This mechanism:\n•Disrupts mitotic spindle formation,', 'in_reply_to_screen_name': 'TheChiefNerd', 'in_reply_to_status_id_str': '1877525664040988721', 'in_reply_to_user_id_str': '69156861', 'is_quote_status': False, 'lang': 'en', 'quote_count': 0, 'reply_count': 0, 'retweet_count': 0, 'retweeted': False, 'user_id_str': '337438166', 'id_str': '1877779600966881577'}}, '_legacy': {'bookmark_count': 0, 'bookmarked': False, 'created_at': 'Fri Jan 10 18:08:38 +0000 2025', 'conversation_id_str': '1877525664040988721', 'display_text